  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": null,
      "what": "This study analyzes driver compliance with lane-level variable speed limits using real-world vehicle trajectory data. It introduces a predictive framework combining Recursive Feature Elimination with a Light Gradient Boosting Machine (LGBM) model, which achieved 93.5% accuracy in predicting compliance. The analysis reveals that compliance is primarily driven by lane speed limits and vehicle type, with lower limits yielding significantly lower compliance rates, especially in the left lane when both lanes share the same limit.",
      "why": "It demonstrates that driver compliance is not uniform across lanes or vehicle types, providing empirical evidence that can be used to design more realistic, static lane-level speed control strategies that account for anticipatory deceleration and lane-specific behaviors."
    }
  },
